In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/activity-lifecycle-audit.ts, add a tie-break to the two ranking sorts so an all-equal primary score can no longer produce identical hot and cold sets.

Measured on the deployed system 2026-09-04. The audit's combined_score has almost no variance: across a live ranked set the values were 0.03, 0.03, 0.03, 0.03, 0.026 — four of five identical. The cause is upstream and separate: the traces view does not project a signature, so distinct_signatures is 0 for every template, signature_affinity is 0, and combined_score collapses to successRate * recency * 0.5 with recency rounding to a single value (0.061 for all).

The consequence is a correctness defect here. Both sorts compare only combined_score, so when scores tie every comparator call returns 0 and a stable sort preserves the input order in BOTH directions. sortedAsc and sortedDesc become the same array, and the top slice and the bottom slice become the SAME TEMPLATES. Verified live: with hotSetSize forced to 2 against a population of 6 — where the slice arithmetic guarantees disjointness because 2 + 2 <= 6 — should_load_hot and should_unload came back with an overlap of 2, i.e. identical.

recent_count is the right tie-break and it has real variance: across the same ranked set its values were 2, 1, 24, 27, 7 — five distinct values for five templates. Among templates whose scores tie, the one exercised more in the recent window is the hotter one.

Find this exact two-line sequence, which appears once in the file:

  const sortedAsc = [...withTraces].sort((a, b) => a.combined_score - b.combined_score);
  const sortedDesc = [...withTraces].sort((a, b) => b.combined_score - a.combined_score);

Replace those two lines with exactly this:

  // Tie-break on recent_count. combined_score is near-constant in practice — measured
  // live as 0.03, 0.03, 0.03, 0.03, 0.026 — because distinct_signatures is 0 for every
  // template (the traces view projects no signature), which zeroes signature_affinity.
  // With an all-equal comparator a stable sort returns the SAME order in both
  // directions, so sortedAsc and sortedDesc were the same array and the top and bottom
  // slices were the same templates: observed as overlap 2 at hotSetSize 2 over a
  // population of 6, where the slice arithmetic alone guarantees disjointness.
  // recent_count carries real variance (measured 2, 1, 24, 27, 7 across five templates)
  // and is the right secondary key: among equally scored templates the one exercised
  // more in the recent window is the hotter one.
  const byRank = (a: TemplateStat, b: TemplateStat) =>
    (a.combined_score - b.combined_score) || (a.recent_count - b.recent_count);
  const sortedAsc = [...withTraces].sort(byRank);
  const sortedDesc = [...withTraces].sort((a, b) => byRank(b, a));

This is a ONE-OP change. Replace only those two adjacent lines. Do not change combined_score itself, do not touch the slice sizing below, do not modify the findings loop, and do not add any import. The comparator is typed against the element type of withTraces — if that array's element type is named something other than TemplateStat in this file, use whatever name that interface actually has rather than inventing one. Change only this one file.

